A1. A good thing to fall asleep on. ~ BORE
A5. This does things in a big way. ~ WHALE
A9. The result of a cold March. ~ CHAP
A13. A good way to get up in the world. ~ ARISE
A14. How many wives are too many wives? ~ ONE
A15. A sure sign of an early fall. ~ PRIDE
A16. The time to lead a fast life. ~ LENT
A17. This supports many a lazy man. ~ STOOL
A19. Epochs. ~ ERAS
A20. You can't conceal her lines with a coat of paint. ~ SHIP
A22. The top of the world. ~ AREA
A24. A crawler. ~ BEETLE
A26. What every preacher knows. ~ PSALMS
A28. The great enemy of S. A. (abbr.) ~ BO
A29. You can't do this on the straight and narrow. ~ REEL
A30. This is a big hearted chap. ~ OTIS
A31. An automatic check writer. ~ PA
A33. A lucky strike. ~ OIL
A35. A gal's name in merrie England. ~ MEG
A36. This is old to the poets. ~ ELD
A37. A long time to wait for a girl. ~ EON
A38. A very desirable figure. ~ SLIM
A40. This is a crack on Broadway. ~ NIFTY
A42. A good sock will often do this. ~ STUN
A43. An Indian fighter. ~ SEPOY
A45. You ought to get this: National Academy of Science. (abbr.) ~ NAS
A46. You'll find a lot of these in a union. (sing.) ~ STATE
A47. A smart sock. ~ RAP
A49. A handy little article. ~ THE
A51. The center of things. (pl.) ~ CORES
A53. What a wife uses to keep many a good man down. ~ THUMB
A56. To deface. ~ MAR
A58. This waters the Scotch. ~ DEE
A59. Little island. ~ AIT
A60. This often drops into a court. ~ LOB
A62. This pertains to flyers. ~ ALAR
A64. These have an itch that cannot be soothed by scratching. ~ PALMS
A66. A trunk. ~ BOLE
A67. You can get a good haul out of these. ~ ROPES
A68. A general. ~ LEE
A69. Fresh tripe. ~ HOOEY
A70. What you're liable to be after a number of balls. ~ TEED
A71. A good place to step when trouble approaches. ~ ASIDE
A72. Biblical name. ~ AMOS

D1. Broken-heart interest. ~ BALM
D2. Rough stuff. ~ ORE
D3. This is a washout. ~ RINSE
D4. Another gal's name. ~ ESTHER
D6. How Greta Garbo feels most of the time. ~ HOT
D7. Soon. ~ ANON
D8. You'll get a roar out of this. ~ LEO
D9. Either these or the anglers are full after a fishing trip. ~ CREELS
D10. You'll find him down on the farm. ~ HIRAM
D11. My God! Another gal's name. ~ ADA
D12. A back-biter. ~ PEST
D17. You display this in a pet. ~ SPLEEN
D18. A pleasing word in an address. ~ LASTLY
D21. This stands by itself. ~ ITEM
D23. A sudden pinch. ~ RAID
D24. An owl that's hooted. ~ BOILED
D25. An old timer. ~ ELGIN
D26. These can be made, despite popular opinion, by young ladies. ~ POETS
D27. Gushers. ~ SPOUTS
D28. The whole cheese. ~ BOSS
D32. This gal was crowned a long time ago. ~ ANNE
D34. This is pressed frequently. ~ LIP
D37. Greek letter. ~ ETA
D39. A head cropper. ~ MORO
D41. This is windy. ~ FAN
D42. This holds up many a rush. ~ STEM
D44. You can find this behind the house. ~ YARD
D46. You'll rarely ever find a speakeasy like this. ~ SHUT
D48. The part of the chicken that doesn't get into the salad. ~ PEEP
D49. Denoting something singled out. ~ THIS
D50. Grownups often play with this kind of set. ~ SMART
D51. An unpleasant hangout. ~ CRAPE
D52. These can be licked easily. ~ SEALS
D53. Domesticated. ~ TAMED
D54. This looks good on a peach. ~ BLOOM
D55. What every rebellious husband finally does. ~ OBEYS
D57. This is bitter medicine. ~ ALOE
D61. A mix-up. ~ OLEO
D63. This should put a stop to a fast pace. ~ RED
D65. A Hawaiian hanging. ~ LEI
D66. Don't let this get a crush on you. ~ BOA


